# `tools/build_standalone.mjs --check` is step 11 of the gate and compares the generated
# standalone brief against a fresh rebuild with plain string equality. The builder emits the
# lines it generates itself with bare LF while inheriting CRLF from the source, and git's
# autocrlf then rewrites every line to CRLF on checkout -- so on Windows the check passes
# immediately after a build and fails after any checkout, though `git diff` shows the content
# identical. Pinning both files to LF makes the comparison hold on every platform.
docs/brief.standalone.html    eol=lf
system/web/brief/index.html   eol=lf

# `tools/harvest.mjs` is step 1 of the gate and rewrites kp.json, math.json and deriv.json on
# every run, emitting bare LF. The blobs are LF, but `core.autocrlf=true` checks them out as
# CRLF -- 981 of them in kp.json alone -- so the first step of the gate leaves three files
# reported modified by `git status` while `git diff` shows no content change at all. A tree that
# is dirty after every green run is a tree in which nobody reads `git status`, which is where a
# real uncommitted change goes unnoticed. Pinning the directory keeps checkout and harvest
# writing the same bytes, and covers whatever the harvest emits next without a second edit here.
data/content/*.json           eol=lf
